This file describes bugs that are not yet fixed and features that are not
yet implemented. Rewritten 2026-10-17 after the v1.1.33 release from a
full read-through of lib/, cli/, daemon/, hub/, snmpd/, the Makefile, CI
and the man pages. Every item cites file:line as of 1.1.33 (ae2af46).
Confidence is "confirmed" (full code path read) unless marked
"plausible" (depends on an OS/library fact not verifiable in-repo).
Items from the previous TODO that are still open are carried over in
section 10; everything previously marked [FIXED] is gone from here and
lives in CHANGELOG.md.

Priority legend: HIGH = wrong or dangerous behaviour reachable in a
normal deployment; MED = incorrect result, data/resource leak or a
documented feature that does not work; LOW = hygiene, edge cases, dead
code, doc drift.

The bhyve/VM track is in TODO2.

=== 1. Blocker: the rootless (1.0.0) model is not actually reachable ===

  [FIXED in 1.1.34] cli/main.cpp refused to start unless euid==0 AND
  uid!=0 (setuid only). Gate removed, HOME from passwd, runtime-dir
  check made non-root tolerant, lite-CI smoke step added.

  [FIXED in 1.1.39] per-user runtime root created AND chowned to the
  operator by crated (every privops connection + new `ensure_user_root`
  verb); rootless jail directories under /var/run/crate/<uid>/
  (RuntimePathsPure::isCrateJailPath for jail_query/console/info/clean);
  network teardown + bridge/auto ipfw through privops
  (RunNet::teardownIface / ipfwExec); healthcheck via JailExec; top via
  query_jail_rctl; console argv split; per-user network_pool split
  (network_pool_user_bits); clear refusals for NAT mode and
  auto_create_bridge under rootless.

* (BLOCKER, design decision needed) an unprivileged `crate run` cannot
  execute anything inside the jail. JailExec::execInJail
  (jail_query.cpp:296-330) forks, jail_attach(2)s, setuid()s and
  execs; jail_attach needs root, and the jexec(8) fallback does too.
  No privops verb performs an exec. So after 1.1.39 a rootless run
  creates the directory, extracts, creates the jail, mounts, wires the
  network — and fails at the first execInJail (lo0 setup, rc.conf,
  the service command itself). Every rootless-only path below this
  line is therefore untested on hardware. Two ways to close it:
    (A) exec_in_jail privops verb with descriptor passing: the client
        sends argv, user and its stdin/stdout/stderr (nvlist_add_
        descriptor over the libnv socket); crated pdfork()s, jail_
        attach(jid), initgroups/setgid/setuid(user), dup2, execv, and
        returns the process descriptor (SCM_RIGHTS) so the client
        waits for exit via kqueue EVFILT_PROCDESC/pdwait without
        pinning a daemon thread. Authz: checkOwnedJid (the jid must be
        the caller's). Keeps the current in-process `crate run`
        architecture intact; execInJail gains a privops branch.
    (B) daemon-run model: rootless `crate run -f` only validates,
        registers the spec (SpecRegistry) and asks crated to start the
        container (control-socket/HTTP `start` already spawns `crate
        run` as root); logs/console come back over the control socket.
        Simpler verbs, but `crate run` stops being the foreground
        supervisor for unprivileged users, and the jid registry records
        the uid-0 run as unowned (needs an owner argument to `start`).
  (A) is the recommendation: it matches the trust model (privops as
  the privileged plane, per-verb ownership gate) and changes no
  operator-visible behaviour. Also needed by `crate console` (jexec
  direct) and `crate update --pkg-only` under rootless.

* (HIGH) remaining direct privileged calls under rootless:
    - run.cpp bridge path: IfconfigOps::createNamedInterface/setUp for
      auto_create_bridge (now a clear ERR under rootless; needs a
      `create_bridge` verb); IpfwOps::deleteNat at teardown (now a
      WARN; needs `delete_ipfw_nat`); host-side static-mac
      (run_net.cpp setMacAddress, now skipped with a WARN; needs a
      host-iface ether field on configure_iface or a `set_iface_mac`
      verb); run.cpp:1595 host v6 epair address (NAT mode only).
    - NAT mode as a whole: Ctx::FwSlots/FwUsers in root-owned
      /var/run/crate (ctx.cpp:191, locs.cpp:11,14), ip.forwarding
      sysctls, setupFirewallRules' ipfw rules partially via privops.
      Now refused up front under rootless. Making NAT mode rootless
      needs the slot allocator in the daemon (an `allocate_fw_slot`/
      `release_fw_slot` pair keyed by peer uid+pid) and a sysctl verb.
    - lib/inter_dns.cpp:36-56 writes /etc/hosts and the unbound
      conf.d file directly (root-only by nature; document, or move the
      rebuild into crated as a verb).
    - lib/info.cpp:129-131 `rctl -h` (limits, not usage — no verb
      returns limits; add a `limits` flag to query_jail_rctl).
    - stack.cpp:787-797 (bridge create), stack.cpp:294 (unbound on
      :53) — stack is root-only today.
    - lib/pfctl_ops.cpp:28 PfLock opens /var/run/crate/pfctl.lock 0600;
      non-root open fails and held() is never checked → no
      serialisation (moot while pf goes through privops, which
      serialises in the daemon).
    - lib/audit.cpp:130-144 writes Config.logs/audit.log (default
      /var/log/crate, not operator-writable) and ignores open()
      failure → audit trail silently lost for every unprivileged
      caller. RuntimePathsPure::perUserAuditLog exists but only
      daemon/audit_per_user.cpp uses it. `logs` is also overridable
      from ~/.config/crate/crate.yml, so a user chooses where their
      own audit trail goes.
    - lib/doctor.cpp:178 (W_OK on /var/run/crate) and :257 (R_OK on
      crated.conf) FAIL for a normal operator; the comment at :171-177
      acknowledges it but keeps FAIL.
    - daemon/routes.cpp spawnCrateRun and control_socket.cpp exec
      `crate run` as uid 0 — the jid registry records those creates as
      unowned. If (B) above is chosen this becomes the main path and
      needs an owner uid carried into create_jail.

=== 2. crated privops / HTTP — security ===

  [FIXED in 1.1.35] the four HIGH items: create_jail `parameters`
  whitelist (PrivOpsPure::validateJailParameters); path-scoped verbs
  confined to the caller's prefix when path_master_prefix is set
  (Decision::DenyUnownedPath); realpath() canonicalisation of every
  path argument in privops_handlers.cpp + lstat/O_EXCL mount targets
  in run_gui.cpp; ws-console on checkBearerAuthFull. Also
  routes.cpp jsonError() escaping.

* (MED, residual of the 1.1.35 canonicalisation) the realpath() check
  and the nmount()/jail -c/devfs call are two steps; a symlink swapped
  in between still wins the race. Closing it needs fd-based operation:
  open(O_RESOLVE_BENEATH) relative to the caller's prefix and
  nmount with "fspath" replaced by an fd ("fd" iov) / jail path via
  fdescfs, or at least a second realpath() after the operation with
  rollback. Same for lib/run_gui.cpp's mount targets (lstat then
  mount). Low practical exposure (needs a compromised jail racing
  its own operator's `crate run`), but the only remaining gap in the
  path chain.

  [FIXED in 1.1.36] viewer token on GET /api/v1/containers, /gui,
  /host; pool gate on GET /exports/:filename
  (TransferPure::artifactContainerName); pf anchor verbs name-scoped
  (Decision::DenyForeignAnchor); attach_zfs/detach_zfs gate the jid;
  jid→owner registry reconciled against live jails at startup and on
  every lookup hit; destroy_jail forgets by pre-resolved jid.

* (MED) Interface-named verbs still host-global
  (lib/privops_authz_pure.cpp default arm): configure_iface gates `jid`
  but not ifname/bridge (any host NIC can be moved into the caller's
  vnet); teardown_iface / bridge_add_member / bridge_del_member and the
  iface of reclaim_iface_from_vnet accept other tenants' epairs. An
  epair half belongs to whoever's create_epair made it, so the fix is
  an iface→owner registry of the same shape as jid_owner_registry:
  record `epairNa`/`epairNb` → uid on a successful create_epair
  (privops_handlers.cpp handleCreateEpair), forget on teardown_iface,
  reconcile against `ifconfig -l`, and gate the four verbs with
  checkOwnedIface (unknown iface → bootstrap Allow, like jid). Non-epair
  ifnames (host NICs) should be denied for per-user callers outright.
  trust-model.md names this as the remaining gap.

* (by design, documented) a pool-scoped ADMIN token can POST
  /imports/:name + /containers/:name/start for a name in its pool and
  have crated run that .crate as root (the spec may declare host
  shares/scripts). trust-model §2b already states admin tokens are
  host-wide, not pool-confined; only viewer/pool-scoped tokens carry
  isolation. Making this real needs the spawned `crate run` to drop to
  the token's mapped uid — part of the rootless §1 work, not a gate.

  [FIXED in 1.1.37] pf rules over a pipe (`pfctl -f -`,
  Util::execCommandWithInput); SO_RCVTIMEO/SO_SNDTIMEO + 64-connection
  cap on privops/control-socket listeners, handshake timeout + session
  cap on ws-console; WsPure::kMaxFramePayload 1 MiB;
  listen.max_body_mb / listen.http_workers for httplib;
  accept4(SOCK_CLOEXEC) + FD_CLOEXEC on the httplib listener;
  control-socket restart = bounded stop + detached spawnCrateRun;
  configure_iface shape check before moveToVnet.

* (MED, residual) privops_listener.cpp `nvlist_recv(connFd, 0)` still
  lets libnv allocate whatever size the untrusted header announces
  (the 1.1.37 timeout + cap bound the thread/fd exhaustion, not the
  per-request allocation). libnv exposes no size cap; options: peek
  the 19-byte nvlist header (magic, version, flags, descriptors, size)
  with MSG_PEEK and refuse > N MiB before nvlist_recv, or move to
  nvlist_unpack over a bounded recv buffer. Needs a FreeBSD build to
  validate against the header layout.

* (MED) Synchronous HTTP handlers remain synchronous — by choice in
  1.1.37 (the worker pool now absorbs them): routes.cpp stop (≤ 11 s),
  restart (stop phase ≤ 11 s, then spawn), export (`tar | xz --extreme`
  inline, minutes). `crate migrate` reads the export response (file,
  size, sha256) and then downloads, so making export asynchronous
  (202 + poll) needs a migrate client change in the same release;
  stop/restart could return 202 after SIGTERM with a detached
  wait/KILL child, but that changes the documented `stopped:true`
  contract. Decide API shape first, then do both ends.

* (LOW) privops_handlers.cpp handleConfigureIface comment describes a
  "move-only" mode (all of ipv4/ipv6/mac empty) that
  validateConfigureIface (privops_pure.cpp:490-491) rejects — either
  allow it in the validator or drop the comment.
* (LOW) Registry write happens after `jail -c` returns (:663-671);
  until then the new jid/path are unowned (bootstrap Allow).
* (LOW) DELETE /api/v1/containers/:name (routes.cpp:348-371) only
  `kill -9` + `jail -r`; crated.8:135-136 says it removes the dataset.
  Returns destroyed:true even when nothing was running. Stop/destroy
  bypass crate's own teardown (mounts, epairs, anchors) → leaks.
* (LOW) daemon/config.cpp:165-176 top-level zfs_master_prefix etc.
  silently override the nested `rootless:` block; `role` is
  unvalidated (a typo such as `Admin` silently demotes to viewer);
  privops_socket_mode gets no tightness warning unlike listen.unix_mode.
* (LOW) handlers at privops_handlers.cpp :171,560,577 return raw
  strerror/e.what() that may echo host paths. (jsonError escaping
  fixed in 1.1.35.)

=== 3. Networking runtime ===

  [FIXED in 1.1.38] bridge/auto pf anchor flushed at teardown; `crate
  clean` sweeps stale crate/* anchors and orphaned v4/v6 pool leases;
  rdr host-port collision warning; `firewall:` policy applied in bridge
  mode (static/pool IP); static-mac hashed from the jail name; ipfw ids
  interleaved (40000+2*jid / 40001+2*jid, jid <= 9999, throttle jid <=
  4999); net.inet6.ip6.forwarding restored. Still open: v4 forwarding is
  restored only when allowOutbound() (run_net.cpp:498, inbound-only
  jails leave it at 1).

  [FIXED in 1.1.39] two users on one host allocated the same
  network_pool IP: per-uid sub-block via PerUserNetPure::composeIpv4
  (network_pool_user_bits, default 8; v6: network_pool6_user_bits 16),
  addresses keep the pool prefix/gateway.

* (MED) MAC portacl rules are host-wide, clobbered per run, never
  removed: run.cpp:724-731 writes security.mac.portacl.rules via
  MacOps::setPortaclRules (mac_ops.cpp:129-145), replacing any other
  container's or the operator's rules; no RunAtEnd restores them.

* (MED) `crate stats` network I/O is always "-" on FreeBSD:
  lib/lifecycle.cpp:142-146 runs `netstat --libxo json`, and when the
  output IS JSON nothing parses it — only the non-JSON fallback branch
  (:147-171) extracts bytes. Drop the libxo attempt or parse it.

* (LOW) run.cpp:1587-1598 IPv6 epair pairs fd00:cra7:e::<100+2n>/126:
  n and n+1 share one /126 on different host interfaces; std::hex of
  > 0xffff yields an invalid literal. (plausible)
* (LOW) spec.cpp:649,652,696 store `ip` / `gateway` / `interface`
  unvalidated (no inet_pton in spec/validate); run_net.cpp:678
  `1u << (32 - prefixLen)` is UB for prefix > 32; run_net.cpp:725
  passes an arbitrary prefixlen token to ifconfig.
* (LOW) run_net.cpp:277,627 substr(5, size-6) throws
  std::out_of_range on an unexpected epair name from privops;
  ipfw_ops.cpp:85 treats EINVAL as "native API available";
  net.cpp:51 indexes netmaskVec[i] without a size check.
* (LOW) mac_ops.cpp:88 addUgidfwRule passes the whole rule as one
  argv token (ugidfw parses per-argv) — would fail; no callers (dead).
  mac_ops.cpp:103-115 deletes by list-line index.
* (LOW) healthcheck: run.cpp:256 `retries: 0` flags UNHEALTHY on
  every failure; `interval: 0` busy-loops at :209 (spec.cpp:1246
  accepts 0).

=== 4. crate stack ===

* (MED) Pool IPs assigned to `crate:` entries are never applied but
  are published in DNS/hosts: stack.cpp:177-192 allocates for every
  entry; only the `spec:` path (:886-908) writes netOpt->staticIp. A
  prebuilt .crate entry gets its own IP while unbound (:250-253) and
  /etc/hosts (:919-925) advertise the phantom pool IP. allocateIpPool
  is in-process only — not persisted, not reconciled with
  network_lease.

* (MED) network_policy rule numbers collide and leak: stack.cpp:398-400
  `static unsigned nextPolicySlot` restarts at 0 in every process →
  every stack uses 30000+; cleanup `ipfw delete 30000` (:466-470)
  removes ALL rules with that number, including another stack's.
  `stack down` never deletes policy rules. otherStackSubnets is never
  passed (:821) → the advertised inter-stack isolation is dead code.
  If IpfwOps::addRule throws mid-loop (`proto` omitted → syntax
  error) rules already added are lost (RunAtEnd built only at :466).
  Host-ipfw `via bridgeN` rules do not see L2 jail<->jail traffic
  unless net.link.bridge.ipfw=1, which nothing sets (plausible).

* (MED) `stack exec` / `stack status` match jails by SUBSTRING:
  stack.cpp:688 and :1070 use j.name.find(e.name); container `db`
  matches jail `mydb-..._pid...` → `stack exec db` runs the command
  as root in the wrong container. Match on the exact nameComponent.

* (MED) mergeSpecs drops overlay fields (spec.cpp:1364-1468): never
  copies cronJobs, diskQuota, securelevel, childrenMax, cpuset, nor a
  false servicesAutoStart. Used by `crate stack` (stack.cpp:487,882)
  and `crate create --template` (cli/main.cpp:151) → a stack/template
  overlay's cron:, limits.disk_quota, security.cpuset are silently
  lost. Add a test that round-trips every Spec field through
  mergeSpecs.

* (LOW) stack.cpp:791 subnet without `/` → find() npos, +1 == 0 →
  "/" + whole subnet appended to the gateway after the bridge was
  already created (no rollback); :307 WEXITSTATUS on a possibly
  signalled status.
* (LOW) `stack down` does not stop containers (:1023-1031 prints
  "scheduled for cleanup"); examples/matrix/README.md:83 tells users
  to run it. Incomplete feature.
* (LOW) stack_pure.cpp:73 caps names at 64 vs NameLimits::kJailName
  128 (same drift as spec_registry below).

=== 5. Spec parsing, config, registries ===

* (MED) Unknown option names are silently dropped in the extended
  `options:` map form: spec.cpp:542-547 collects names into `opts`,
  :548 iterates only allOptionsLst, so `options: {nett: {...}}` never
  reaches spec.options and validate()'s unknown-option check
  (spec_pure.cpp:222) never fires. The list form IS caught.

* (MED) Documented `securelevel: -1` / `enforce_statfs` cannot be
  parsed: spec.cpp:866,880 use Util::toUInt, which rejects a leading
  `-` (util_pure.cpp:78). crate.5:166 and README.md:51 document -1;
  the -1 branches at spec.cpp:881 and spec_pure.cpp:345 are dead.

* (MED) Spec-registry name rule contradicts NameLimits:
  spec_registry_pure.cpp:32 caps at 63 and :36 forbids `.`, while
  WarmPure::validateJailName allows 128 chars and `.`. A valid jail
  `my.app` or a 64+ char name makes upsert throw; run.cpp:955-961 only
  WARNs, so the jail starts but SpecRegistry::lookup (lifecycle.cpp
  :488) and the daemon's PostStart cannot find its spec. Switch to
  NameLimits::kJailName + the validateJailName charset.

* (MED) Spec-registry lock-on-renamed-inode race: spec_registry.cpp:56
  flocks the fd of effectivePath(), but writeAllAtomic (:98-122)
  renames a fresh inode over it, and the .tmp name is fixed —
  identical to the network_lease defect carried over in section 10.
  Fix both with one stable `<path>.lock` file.

* (MED) Config partial application + dead keys: config.cpp:41-114
  applyYaml throws at :63/:73 (network_pool6 / firewall_backend) or on
  any as<>() type mismatch; load() catches per file, so keys before
  the bad one apply and keys after are skipped (WARN only) — tier
  precedence then depends on key order. Unknown keys are ignored.
  `securelevel` / `children_max` (config.cpp:80-81, documented
  crate.5:649-652) have no consumer anywhere (grep) — dead config.

* (MED) util_pure.cpp:198 fileExists `st_mode & S_IFREG`, :203
  dirExists `& S_IFDIR` — bitmask instead of S_ISREG/S_ISDIR; a UNIX
  socket (0140000) satisfies both. Same bug 1.1.27 fixed in
  util.cpp:685. Affects Config::resolveCrateFile, run.cpp:473 etc.

* (MED) Ports / counts without range checks: net inbound-tcp/udp
  (spec.cpp:583-594 via SpecPure::parsePortRange) accept 0, > 65535
  and reversed `90-80` (validate only compares spans, spec_pure.cpp
  :235); gui/vnc_port (:1197) and mac_portacl/allow_ports (:1114)
  unchecked; healthcheck.interval: 0 (:1246) → tight jexec loop;
  restart.delay: 0 → tight restart loop (cli/main.cpp:229).

* (MED) `restart: always` loops forever after Ctrl-C / `crate stop`:
  cli/main.cpp:194-230 — on SIGINT/SIGTERM runCrate returns
  returnCode = 128+sig (run.cpp:1953) with succ=true; the loop checks
  only `policy`, never the signal, despite the comment at :202.
  Policy typos (`on_failure`) silently mean "run once"; no backoff;
  unless-stopped == always. Validate the policy enum at parse time.

* (LOW) spec.cpp:667,708 `vlan:` non-integer → as<int>() throws
  YAML::TypedBadConversion, surfaced as "internal error
  (std::exception)" (main.cpp:344) rather than a spec error.
* (LOW) spec.cpp:479-494 `files:` silently ignores unknown sub-keys
  and a non-sequence `share` (the `dirs:` twin errors at :473/:476);
  :1076-1088 firewall/allow-tcp|udp with a map value silently ignored;
  :1109-1120 mac_portacl non-map / allow_ports non-sequence ignored;
  :938-942 `x11: maybe` silently ignored.
* (LOW) validate_pure.cpp:83,90,245,249 recommend x11/mode=headless,
  which the parser rejects (spec.cpp:924); :108 tests gui/mode ==
  "shared", not a parseable value (dead branch).
* (LOW) run.cpp:1819-1821 cron schedule/command written verbatim — a
  YAML block scalar with embedded newlines adds crontab lines;
  schedule format unvalidated (user is).
* (LOW) audit.cpp:140-142 relies on single-write atomicity <= PIPE_BUF
  but argv (audit_pure.cpp:83) is unbounded; write return ignored →
  interleaved/partial JSONL lines under concurrency.
* (LOW) util_pure.cpp:146 `$HOME` alone (size==5) not substituted;
  `$HOMER/x` → <home>R/x.
* (LOW) Duplicate top-level keys: only `scripts` is checked
  (spec.cpp:1336); other sections are processed twice (lists append)
  or last-wins. (plausible — yaml-cpp iteration semantics)
* (LOW) locs.cpp:18-22 static initialiser calls getSysctlString; a
  throw there is std::terminate before main.
* (LOW) lib/doctor.cpp: :507 dead jailXname; :77 WARNs when ipfw is
  missing on pf-only hosts; `pf` not in kModules; :204/:317 hardcode
  /var/log/crate ignoring Config.logs; :327 threshold is 100 MB while
  the comment/section say 50.

=== 6. crate doctor ===

* (HIGH) Wrong absolute paths → false FAIL on every FreeBSD host:
  lib/doctor.cpp:129 requires /usr/bin/jls (real /usr/sbin/jls,
  pathnames.h:96) and :134/:425 use /sbin/rctl (real /usr/bin/rctl,
  pathnames.h:75). `crate doctor` always reports a required-command
  FAIL (exit 2) and always skips the RCTL presence check. Use the
  CRATE_PATH_* constants.
* (HIGH) doctor.cpp:73 probes racct with `kldstat -n racct`; RACCT is
  a kernel option, not a module, so this is WARN on every host and
  the exit code is permanently >= 1. Check the sysctl
  kern.racct.enable instead. (plausible — FreeBSD fact)

=== 7. Lifecycle, storage, GUI, monitoring, DNS, VPN ===

* (HIGH) `crate snapshot restore` NULL-derefs libzfs:
  lib/zfs_ops.cpp:84 calls zfs_rollback(zhp, nullptr, B_FALSE) with
  the SNAPSHOT handle as the first argument; the signature is
  zfs_rollback(zfs_handle_t *fs, zfs_handle_t *snap, boolean_t force)
  and libzfs dereferences `snap` unconditionally → SIGSEGV before the
  exec fallback can run. Requires HAVE_LIBZFS, which port/Makefile:50
  sets for the FreeBSD port. Open the parent dataset for `fs`, the
  snapshot for `snap`. Same shape at :349: zfs_show_diffs(zhp, outFd,
  fromsnap, tosnap, flags) is passed snap2 as fromsnap and nullptr as
  tosnap, so with two snapshots it shows snap2 → live filesystem, not
  snap1 → snap2 (plausible — libzfs API semantics).

* (HIGH) `crate export` archives everything mounted inside the jail
  root: lib/export.cpp:103 `tar cf - -C <jailPath> .` with no
  --one-file-system / exclusions. `crate run` nullfs-mounts host
  dirs/share, files/share, X11/Wayland/Pulse sockets and devfs under
  J(...) (run.cpp:752,1757,1791,1802; run_gui.cpp:316,739,828). A spec
  sharing $HOME exports the user's host directory into a shareable
  .crate (data leak + bloat + /dev nodes packed). create.cpp unmounts
  before tar (:541-543); export has no equivalent. README:18 and
  crate.5:82 carry no caveat. Fix: --one-file-system plus explicit
  --exclude of every share destination.

* (HIGH) `crate inter-dns` never emits any crate jail (feature dead):
  the kernel jail name is <name>_pid<pid> (run.cpp:907, run_jail.cpp
  :41-104); inter_dns.cpp:78 drops any name failing
  InterDnsPure::validateHostname, and inter_dns_pure.cpp:22-27 rejects
  `_`. collectEntries() is always empty → /etc/hosts gets an empty
  marker block and crate.conf only the local-zone. Strip the `_pid`
  suffix (nameComponent) before validation. Once fixed, multi-IP jails
  still break the output: jail_query.cpp:115,195 store ip4.addr via
  jailparam_export, which comma-joins arrays, so inter_dns_pure.cpp
  :64,78 emit `10.0.0.1,10.0.0.2` into hosts/local-data and unbound
  rejects the file.

  [FIXED in 1.1.39] `crate console`: "login -f <user>" was one argv
  element to jexec; split into words, extra args appended verbatim.
  Still root-only (jexec) until the exec verb from §1 exists.

* (MED) Auto-incremental backup/replicate chain is corrupt after any
  failure or mixed use: backup.cpp:68 takes the snapshot BEFORE
  `zfs send`; on send failure the @backup-<ts> snapshot persists and
  pickLatestBackupSuffix (zfs_dataset.cpp:27-39) later selects it as
  the incremental base although no stream holds it → unrestorable
  .inc-from-X file / remote "incremental source does not match".
  `crate backup` and `crate replicate` share the backup- namespace,
  so a local backup silently becomes the base of the next remote
  increment. BackupPure::buildDestroySnapshotArgv (backup_pure.cpp
  :130) has no caller — nothing ever destroys backup snapshots. `zfs
  list -r` (zfs_dataset.cpp:31) includes descendants and the regex
  (zfs_dataset_pure.cpp:18) does not pin the dataset. warm.cpp:48-56
  likewise leaves the snapshot behind if `zfs clone` fails.

* (MED) backup-prune mis-classifies chained incrementals as orphans
  and `--delete-orphans` deletes them: backup_prune_pure.cpp:262-286
  builds keptSuffixes only from kept FULLS, but --auto-incremental
  bases each run on the latest backup-* snapshot, i.e. the previous
  incremental; inc2 (inc-from-<inc1>) is always "orphan" even when F
  and inc1 are kept. tests/unit/backup_prune_pure_test.cpp:295-343
  only covers incrementals based on a full.

* (MED) `crate update --pkg-only` cannot work on crates built with
  `pkg:`: create.cpp:188 runs `pkg delete -f pkg`, :347-350 removes
  /var/db/pkg, and rmdirHierExcept(/usr/sbin) drops /usr/sbin/pkg;
  update.cpp:72 then jexec's /usr/sbin/pkg upgrade → ENOENT (or, after
  a bootstrap, an empty package DB). CHANGELOG 0.8.41 presents this as
  the supported upgrade path. Either keep pkg + its DB when the spec
  installs packages, or make update bootstrap pkg and re-register.

* (MED) ZfsOps::send/recv exec fallback silently succeeds on fork()
  failure (zfs_ops.cpp:385-399, 413-427): pid == -1 matches neither
  branch; restoreCrate then prints "recovered" with nothing received;
  waitpid return unchecked. lzc_receive(targetDataset) (:402-411)
  needs a fs@snap name; `--to pool/jails/name` always yields EINVAL,
  so the "native libzfs" label printed at backup.cpp:110 is never
  true (plausible).

* (MED) `crate restart` drops the original run's CLI options:
  lifecycle.cpp:512-518 forwards only runCrateFile and logProgress;
  --name / --warm-base / --var are lost, so the container comes back
  under a different name/config. stopCrate also returns true after
  destroy_jail soft-fails (:431-452), so restart proceeds while the old
  jail still exists; `crate stop` exits 0 with "stopped" in that case.

* (MED) `crate clean` races an in-progress `crate run`:
  clean.cpp:50-78 rmdirHier's every jail-* directory not in
  JailQuery::getAllJails(); run.cpp:317 creates and populates
  jail-<name>-<hex> BEFORE jail_set, so a concurrent clean deletes a
  container mid-extraction (plausible — timing). chroot-create-* dirs
  (create.cpp:441) are never swept.

* (MED) Predictable world-writable /tmp scratch files (same class as
  the 1.1.25 gui.cpp fix): run_gui.cpp:201 /tmp/crate-vncpw-<N>, :449
  /tmp/crate-xorg-<N>.conf, :911 /tmp/crate-clipboard-<name>.sh;
  display N is global (gui_registry.cpp:60 starts at 10).
  Util::Fs::writeFile is O_NOFOLLOW|O_TRUNC so symlinks are blocked,
  but a pre-created attacker-owned 0666 regular file is truncated and
  written: the VNC password lands in the attacker's file; the Xorg
  conf has no chmod so ModulePath/Load can be rewritten between write
  and execl (:462); a FIFO blocks open() forever. Use mkstemp /
  mkdtemp as gui.cpp now does.

* (MED) Unauthenticated VNC bound to all interfaces by default:
  run_gui.cpp:189-195 runs x11vnc `-shared -forever -nopw` with no
  -localhost; vnc_server.cpp:141 never sets listenInterface
  (libvncserver default INADDR_ANY). crate.5:270-277 documents no
  exposure caveat, while the compositor path (:1087-1100) defaults to
  127.0.0.1 with a warning. Default to loopback (reuse gui.vnc_bind).

* (MED) Migrate token IS visible in `ps`: migrate.cpp:73-74 puts
  `Authorization: Bearer <token>` in curl's argv (the comment admits
  it); README.md:144 claims it never appears on the command line.
  Use `-H @file` / --config. migrate_pure.cpp:146 also accepts explicit
  http:// (plaintext token), and the artifact is uploaded without
  checking the sha256 the export response carries (migrate.cpp:81,164).

* (MED) `crate top` DISK column is write bandwidth, not usage:
  top_pure.cpp:148 maps `writebps` to diskBytes, rendered as "DISK
  352.5 MB" (README.md:274-278). cputime from rctl is whole seconds,
  so at 1 Hz cpuPercent (top_pure.cpp:64-69) quantises to 0/100/200%.

* (LOW) lifecycle.cpp:272 logsTarget unvalidated →
  `crate logs ../../x` reads /var/log/x/console.log (unprivileged
  read; minor); JSON at :180-189 emits jail->name / rctl values
  unescaped (non-crate jails can have arbitrary names). Follow mode
  never notices rotation/truncation.
* (LOW) throttle.cpp:98-141 `--clear --show` clears, then errors
  "at least one of --ingress..."; retune.cpp:41-42 `--clear KEY`
  alone is rejected; retune_pure.cpp:35 lists `cpuset`, not an RCTL
  resource; isHumanSize accepts 1.5M which rctl rejects;
  parseHumanSize (:127) can overflow long and is unused.
* (LOW) clean.cpp:232-244 deletes orphan throttle RULES (20000+) but
  never the dummynet PIPES (kPipeBase 10000+); the comment at :195
  says pipes live at 20000..29999 — wrong, that is kRuleBase.
  clean.cpp:97-98 iface-record liveness uses path.find(name)
  substring match.
* (LOW) import.cpp:262,272 outFile == archivePath is a raw string
  compare (`-o foo.crate` on ./foo.crate passes, copy_file fails);
  ImportPure::archiveHasTraversal (import_pure.cpp:21) rejects any
  entry containing `..` (e.g. foo..bar); import re-runs the full
  decrypt+decompress pipeline 3x.
* (LOW) export.cpp:52 prefix match jail-<target>- makes `web` resolve
  jail-web-2-xxxx; first hit wins. snmpd/collector.cpp:66 and
  stack.cpp (section 4) have the same substring/prefix bug.
* (LOW) zfs_ops.cpp:362 ::write return ignored; mount.cpp:61 strdup
  NULL unchecked; create.cpp:50-59 guessCrateName derefs
  *runServices.rbegin() when both cmd and services are empty
  (plausible — depends on validate ordering); create.cpp:564 chowns
  output to getuid() (under sudo that is root).
* (LOW) autoname_pure.cpp:16,24 std::gmtime (non-reentrant) — safe in
  the CLI, unsafe if ever called from the threaded daemon.
* (LOW) jail_query.cpp:141 fallback parses `jls -N`, whose first
  column is the NAME → jid always 0, rest misparsed; breaks on paths
  with spaces. :324,384 a command legitimately exiting 126 is mistaken
  for jail_attach failure and re-executed via jexec (double
  execution); :309-310,360-361 ignore setgid/setuid return → runs as
  root in the jail if they fail.
* (LOW) vnc_server.cpp:67 XGetImage with start-time dimensions; after
  `gui resize` (gui.cpp:402) BadMatch hits Xlib's default handler
  which exit()s the whole `crate run` (no XSetErrorHandler) →
  RunAtEnd teardown skipped. (plausible)
* (LOW) run_gui.cpp:1187-1194 copyX11Auth still copies host
  .Xauthority/.ICEauthority for gui.mode compositor/none/wayland;
  :878,889 setupClipboard reads only x11Options, ignoring gui.mode
  nested; :227-228 fork failure leaves the vncpw file behind; :1084
  `vnc: true` with backend drm silently ignored; wayvnc's 127.0.0.1 is
  the jail's loopback in a vnet jail so the advertised port (:1157) is
  unreachable from the host.
* (LOW) gui.cpp:171-172,326-330 comments claim the command "runs as
  root / registry is root-only" — stale post-1.0.0.
* (LOW) inspect.cpp:170 started_at always 0; :57-68 ip6/mac never
  populated; inspect_pure.cpp:154-159 mount parsing breaks on paths
  containing " on " or " (".
* (LOW) list_pure.cpp:67-76, info.cpp:103 print kernel host.hostname
  raw to the terminal; allow.set_hostname is spec-controllable
  (run_jail.cpp:134) → a jail can inject escape sequences into the
  operator's `crate list`. JSON paths are escaped.
* (LOW) capsicum_ops.cpp enterCapabilityMode / initCapDns /
  resolveDns / limitFdRights have no callers (only
  initCapSyslog/logSyslog via audit.cpp); initCapDns leaks g_capdns
  on repeat calls; unused WARN macro. drm_session.cpp:18,53,63 single
  global g_deviceId — a second openDevice orphans the first.
* (LOW) migrate.cpp:155-156 workdir /tmp/crate-migrate-<pid> is
  predictable (mkdir throws on EEXIST → DoS only).

=== 8. crate-hub ===

* (HIGH) Container data path is dead end-to-end. crated's
  GET /api/v1/containers (daemon/routes.cpp:134-138) returns
  {"status":"ok","data":<jls --libxo json>} — an OBJECT; the hub
  stores the whole envelope as status.containers (hub/poller.cpp
  :143). AggregatorPure::countTopLevelObjects (aggregator_pure.cpp
  :40) requires [...] and returns 0, so /api/v1/aggregate,
  /api/v1/datacenters and /api/v1/scheduling/least-loaded
  (hub/api.cpp:116,135,201) always see 0 containers on every node
  (scheduler always a pure tie, confidence 50); /api/v1/containers
  (api.cpp:74) embeds the envelope and hub/web/app.js:124 then
  JSON.parse()s an already-parsed object → throws → "No containers"
  forever. tests/unit/hub_aggregator_pure_test.cpp:75-99 feeds only
  synthetic [{...}]. Fix on the hub side: unwrap data and
  jail-information.jail.

* (HIGH) Daemonised crate-hub never polls: hub/main.cpp:210 starts
  the poller thread, then :220 fork()s; the child has only the forking
  thread, so no poll loop runs in the default (non -f) mode. At
  shutdown :239 pollerThread.join() in the child joins a thread that
  does not exist in that process (std::system_error / UB). Fork
  before starting threads.

* (HIGH) Dashboard action buttons target the wrong host:
  hub/web/app.js:168-172 take the node host from cells[1] of the Nodes
  table, but since the Datacenter column was inserted (index.html:42,
  app.js:88-92) cells[1] is the datacenter → every
  start/stop/restart/destroy goes to https://default/... app.js:49,52
  also hard-code https:// while crated's TCP listener and the hub's
  own polling are plain HTTP by default, and crated emits no CORS
  headers (grep Access-Control in daemon/: none) — so the cross-origin
  fetch would be blocked anyway. Route actions through the hub.

* (MED) Hub API fully unauthenticated, bound to 0.0.0.0
  (hub/main.cpp:230), undocumented as such: no auth middleware in
  hub/api.cpp; POST /api/v1/admin/prune (api.cpp:213) deletes data and
  is CSRF-able from any website; crate-hub.8 has no
  AUTHENTICATION/SECURITY section; /api/v1/nodes leaks node host:port
  and error strings.

* (MED) Node polling has no server-cert verification path and sends
  tokens in cleartext by default: hub/poller.cpp:112-117 uses TLS only
  when tls_cert (a CLIENT cert) is configured; no set_ca_cert_path /
  enable_server_certificate_verification, so an HTTPS crated with a
  server-only cert cannot be polled, and the default path sends the
  admin Bearer token (:122) over plain HTTP. poller.cpp also never
  defines CPPHTTPLIB_OPENSSL_SUPPORT (daemon/server.cpp:10 does), so
  httplib::SSLClient at :113 will not compile once a hub build target
  exists.

* (MED) Hand-built JSON with unescaped interpolation:
  hub/api.cpp:33-37,55,74,98 splice s.name, s.host, s.lastError and
  the raw node body into JSON/Prometheus output (JsonPure is used by
  ha/scheduling but not here). One node answering 200 with a non-JSON
  body (reverse-proxy error page) makes /api/v1/containers invalid
  JSON for the whole cluster.

* (MED) crate-hub.8 CONFIGURATION contradicts the code: man :81-94
  documents listen_port, separate host:/port: keys and
  --foreground/--config; the code reads only `host` as host:port
  (poller.cpp:52,106-108, default 9800), ignores `port` and
  `listen_port` (port only via -p, main.cpp:177), accepts only short
  flags (:175-178). Omits datacenter, tls_cert/tls_key, ha:,
  /api/v1/datacenters, /api/v1/ha/orders,
  /api/v1/scheduling/least-loaded and the `schedule` subcommand.

* (MED) Poller writes shared state outside the mutex:
  poller.cpp:146,149 touch status.lastError / hostInfo / containers
  without statusMutex_ while getNodeStatuses() (:160-163) copies under
  it → data race on std::string. On a down node `containers` keeps the
  last good body and recordPoll (:149) still inserts it each cycle →
  stale data presented as fresh with no flag.

* (MED) IPv6 node hosts cannot be configured: poller.cpp:106-108
  splits on the last `:` → [::1]:9800 yields host "[::1]" and bare
  ::1 yields host ":" + port 1; stoi failures mark the node
  permanently down with lastError="stoi".

* (LOW) /api/v1/nodes omits `datacenter` (api.cpp:33-38) so the Nodes
  table always shows "default" (app.js:91). main.cpp:177 stoi on -p
  uncaught; :206 SQLite open throws if /var/db/crate-hub/ is absent
  (nothing creates it); :229-231 listen() failure is silent and main
  sleeps forever. store.cpp: one sqlite3* shared by poller and HTTP
  threads with no mutex (safe only with SQLITE_THREADSAFE=1 —
  plausible). poller.cpp:51 n["host"].as<string>() throws on a node
  without `host` and silently drops all later nodes; :125,140 a down
  node costs two 5 s timeouts so N down nodes exceed poll_interval.
  api.cpp:164 emits HA failover orders from the hub's own view only
  (hub-side partition → orders while the primary is healthy;
  mitigated because `crate migrate` needs the source).

=== 9. crate-snmpd ===

* (HIGH) Spins at 100% CPU and never reconnects after the master
  closes the socket: snmpd/mib.cpp:410-414 — on EOF select() is
  readable immediately, readN returns 0, dispatchOnce returns false,
  the main loop (snmpd/main.cpp:84-93) calls it again with no delay;
  g_agentxFd stays >= 0, no reconnect/backoff exists. A
  bsnmpd/snmpd restart permanently kills monitoring and pins a core.

* (HIGH) AgentX session ID never taken from the Open response:
  mib.cpp:221-223 sets g_sessionId = g_transactionId (=1) instead of
  header bytes 4-7 of the Response. Register/Close then carry a
  sessionId the master did not assign → RFC 2741 §7.1.1 notOpen;
  registration fails with any master that does not hand out 1
  (plausible); protocol violation confirmed; only logged (:255).

* (MED) OctetString length overflow → out-of-bounds read:
  mib_pure.cpp:116-119 — n is uint32_t, (n + 3) wraps for
  n >= 0xFFFFFFFD → padded = 0 → bounds check passes → out.assign(ptr,
  n) reads ~4 GB past the buffer. Reachable via the
  NON_DEFAULT_CONTEXT string (:145-148). mib.cpp:420 and :151 also
  allocate payloadLen bytes with no cap (bad_alloc uncaught). Requires
  a hostile/buggy master on the local socket.

* (MED) Little-endian flag honoured only in the header:
  mib_pure.cpp:83-88 flips header fields, but decodeOid (:108) and
  decodeOctetString (:116) always use loadU32be; mib_pure.h:78-79
  claims LE is handled.

* (MED) CRATE-MIB table and traps are not implemented but documented
  as the product: mib.cpp:371-374 answers noSuchInstance for every
  non-scalar OID; resolveGetNext (:380-387) walks only 4 scalars;
  sendTrap (:316-319) only logs. snmpd/CRATE-MIB.txt:65-165 defines an
  11-column table plus NOTIFICATION-TYPEs; README.md:761-763 says the
  agent "exposes container metrics". collector.h:20 says "CPU% x 100"
  but collector.cpp:52 stores raw percent; netRx/netTx/uptimeTicks
  never populated.

* (LOW) mib.cpp:263-287 registers root + two overlapping child
  subtrees at equal priority (duplicateRegistration, logged only);
  :430-437 answers Response PDUs with a Response; :449 write() return
  unchecked and SIGPIPE not ignored; :139,152 short read() not looped.
  mib.cpp:90 g_version = "0.8.48" and port/Makefile:2
  PORTVERSION=0.8.48 vs 1.1.33 (cli/args.cpp:756). main.cpp:41-48
  `-i 0` runs jls/rctl every 100 ms.

=== 10. Carried over from the previous TODO (still open, unchanged) ===

* (correctness, FreeBSD-only) lib/run.cpp exception-path teardown
  order diverges from the success path: the happy path tears down in
  an explicit doNow() order (jail → mounts → net → fw-slot,
  ~:1942-1984); on a throw before that block, RunAtEnd destructors
  fire in reverse-declaration order — network teardowns (~:1051-1068)
  run BEFORE destroyJail (:958), mounts unmount last, removeMacRules
  (:669) runs against an already-recycled jid. Cleanup quality on an
  already-failing run. Fix: one ordered teardown for both paths.
  Validate on a real FreeBSD host (docs/on-hardware-validation.md).

* (correctness, MED) lib/run.cpp registers several teardown handlers
  AFTER the operations that can throw: NetworkLease::allocateFor
  succeeds (~:1153) but configureStaticIp/copyFile throws before
  releaseLeaseAtEnd is set → lease row stays forever (see also 3.
  "leases never reclaimed"); same for IPv6 (~:1184); bridge
  auto-created (~:1090) then setUp/createBridgeEpair throws before
  destroyBridgeEpairAtEnd.reset (~:1115) → bridge + epair leak;
  run_net.cpp createBridgeEpair / the extras loop (~:1664) leak an
  epairN pair per failed attempt. Register the RunAtEnd immediately
  after each successful acquisition.

* (correctness/security, MED) lib/network_lease.cpp +
  network_lease6.cpp flock the lease FILE's inode but writeAllAtomic
  renames a fresh inode over the path → a second waiter holds a lock
  on the unlinked inode and reads stale contents → two concurrent
  `crate run` by the same user allocate the SAME address. Same bug in
  spec_registry.cpp (section 5). Fix with a stable `<path>.lock`.

  [FIXED in 1.1.36] jid→owner registry forget-by-name: destroy_jail
  now resolves name→jid before the handler runs and forgets by jid;
  stale rows are reconciled against live jails.

* (correctness, MED) lib/stack.cpp `stack up` calls runCrate()
  IN-PROCESS and sequentially per container (stack.cpp:871,977): a
  service-only first container never returns, so the second is never
  started. Spawn each `crate run` like daemon/routes.cpp spawnCrateRun.

  [FIXED in 1.1.37] fd hygiene part 2: accept4(SOCK_CLOEXEC) on the
  three hand-rolled listeners, FD_CLOEXEC on the httplib listening
  sockets via set_socket_options.

* (correctness, MED) cron: all entries go into ONE crontab,
  /var/cron/tabs/<user of the FIRST entry> (run.cpp:1823); a `user:`
  on a later entry is silently ignored. Documented as a limitation in
  crate.5 (1.1.31). Group by user, one crontab per user.

  [FIXED in 1.1.37] connection-fd recv/send timeouts and a per-listener
  concurrency cap on the privops and control-socket planes.

* (correctness, LOW) lib/privops_wire_pure.cpp escape() is the one
  JSON escaper not on JsonPure: passes control bytes through raw. Its
  decoder (~:60) lacks \uXXXX, so teach the decoder first (round-trip
  test), then delegate.

* (LOW batch) daemon/routes.cpp:1014 import tmp file
  <name>.crate.tmp.<pid> is constant across threads (two concurrent
  imports of one name interleave; no fsync before rename);
  execPipelineImpl fork() failure for child i>0 throws without
  reaping children 0..i-1; capture loops treat read()==-1 as EOF
  (EINTR); jail_query.cpp ignores waitpid failure / decodes an
  uninitialised status; ws_console.cpp select() with fds >= FD_SETSIZE;
  run.cpp hcThread joinable while execInJail can throw on fork EAGAIN
  → std::terminate skips every RunAtEnd; stack.cpp inet_pton results
  unchecked for ip_range.

* (consistency, LOW) hub/ha_pure isValidName caps names at 64; switch
  to NameLimits::kJailName when the hub gets a build target.

* (security, HIGH-if-reachable, latent) libvirt VM track
  (lib/vm_spec.cpp:97-184 generateDomainXml embeds name / disk /
  bridge / hostPath / tag unescaped into domain XML handed to
  virDomainCreateXML as root; vm_run.cpp:250-388 and vm_stack.cpp:88
  build root-written paths from vmName / vol.tag with no traversal or
  newline check; memoryToKiB val*1024*1024 overflow). No caller
  today (built only WITH_LIBVIRT). Decision 2026-10: kept as roadmap.
  XML-escape / validate every field BEFORE wiring it up.

